t dogs and cats  have pain faces but what about other  species and can we use it to score pain  well here's some data from again from  one of yvette's studies uh two mice are  both undergone vasectomy both received  analgesics  the mouse on the left just looking at  pain faces i would say  he's doing okay  i still might want to look at him again  and  check that he doesn't need more  analgesics but the one on the right  of my right anyway  is showing orbital tightening  has got some cheek bulging some nose  bulging  and i think needs a top up with either  more carpro fan or a different analgesic  so to answer the question do animals  have pain faces yes they do  can we use them for cage side assessment  yet  we can start but what we have to do is  go into this with our our eyes wide open  we're going to see a string of  publications over the next year or so  dealing with pain faces in macaques in  dogs and cats in horses in pigs and  sheep perhaps even in fish  and in mouse rat rabbit  but what we know in certainly these  species  is that there are numerous other factors  that can affect facial expression  that sedation can be a major confound  and  infuriatingly there are  different expressions of  degree of grimacing in different mouse  strains  so i don't think it's going to prove to  be the gold standard for assessing pain  but i do think it's going to be a really  useful technique i want to illustrate  what i mean about the confounding  factors again  thanks to yvette with some data from her  thesis  these are three strains of mice valve  c's c3he's and c57 black sixes  on the left we've got a group  who've had vasectomy surgeries  on the right  mice who've just had an anaesthetic  and what we can see is that our balb  seas are both pulling what look like  pain faces  our c3hes who've had surgery are pulling  a pain face  but it's not so obvious uh in our c3hgs  have just had an aesthetic and our bowel  seas  are doing pretty much nothing  so what you need to know  just as with pain behaviors  you need to know how your strain of  mouse behaves normally you need to do  the same  with pain faces and look at some control  mice and see what's happening  and hopefully both yvette  and amy miller who's  working in our lab  will publish to explain how you can  approach this problem and how you can  tackle it  finally i want to mention some really  interesting new metrics that again might  be translatable to cage side assessments  and these have come out of a  dissatisfaction from the pain research  community that the standard old measures  of nociceptive testing of hot plate and  tail flick and so forth  weren't proving particularly successful  in developing new drugs particularly for  chronic and neuropathic pain  and  there have been moves in assessing these  types of pain in people to move away  from pain questionnaires and visual  analog scores to developing sort of  quality of life scoring of looking at  how well people function did they get up  were they motivated to get up to go out  to socialize to get dressed to wash to  keep house and so forth  and are the parallels in rodents  and could that be building a nest  socializing with your cage mates  maintaining a burrow and performing  other highly motivated behaviors  and i think the probably the first step  in developing this was the  use of burrowing behavior which was  proposed as a measure of general  uh well-being in rodents  and the initial paper showed that it was  influenced by viral and bacterial  infections and by cns lesions  and then it was demonstrated  in 2011  that rats with peripheral nerve injury  with neuropathic pain  and with inflammatory pain reduced their  digging behavior  and this was digging um  gravel out of a test bowl  and that if you gave analgesics you  could reverse that normalize the digging  and just recently we have another  publication  looking at this in joint pain and i'm  sure more will follow  we also have data in mouse  from pauline jerkoff in switzerland  and sorry i'm trying to pick up a  question and it's disappeared but i'll  pick it up later  um and a quick apology uh that i spelt  her name correctly here but not on the  next slide  she's looked at latency to borrow as  well as  weight of pellets  post surgery and in other circumstances  in mouse and this is some of her data  from this paper  spelt  one f not two if you want to go and  download it  this is latency to borrow in hours  in mice who've either had surgery  surgery plus pain relief  or the appropriate anaesthetic controls  and what you're seeing uh compared to  baseline which is an orange  is that all of these procedures  decrease um the  increase the time taken to borrow  but giving analgesics  to mice have had surgery reduces that  latency significantly  i urge you to read the full paper and  the other publications from this group  which developed this whole idea uh very  clearly and what i would say is that all  you need is a bottle  and some pellets of diet  to try this out  and once again you need to know does my  strain of mouse burrow  how quickly does it burrow and can i use  this as a quick test of its perhaps we  should say its well-being  the group in switzerland have also  looked at nest building as have some  other groups  and once again like other measures  nest buildings affected by other factors  and at the moment what we'd say is that  if we see a mouse that was building a  well-formed nest and it no longer does  that  um  then it needs your attention and it  could be pain or it could be something  else  what do i propose about the personal  resective mice um again i'll come back  to that later i'm trying to pick up  questions as we go um because i know  some of you will dash off to other  sessions  but i'll try and catch up  with all of these shortly  so  nest building if we're providing  nestlets as environmental enrichment  and we're looking at our mice then we  can apply some of those assessments  uh perhaps borrowing is something we can  incorporate as a measure and we need to  know what other factors affect it  how it varies between strains and what's  the best way of doing this assessment  but i'm really optimistic i think we've  already overcome  major misconceptions about the nature of  pain in animals  the responses to pain in animals  what analgesics we can use and the  relative importance of their side  effects  and how about the progress we've made  the real progress in terms of how many  rats and mice and i'm focusing on  rodents because they're the species we  know from these literature reviews are  least likely to get analgesics  how well are we doing with them and this  again is robert's data  which i hope he's going to publish uh  shortly  uh he repeated the 2005 6  literature analysis and added in 2012 to  13 using the same uh  literature review strategy for selecting  the papers  and we've improved over those that time  course but we still only have 22 percent  of those of those publications  specifically saying they gave an  analgesic post surgery  we have another 27  in which the analgesic that was used uh  sorry the anaesthetic that was used had  an analgesic component  but we're getting nearly half those  animals  not receiving  anything  now  why is that well i think one of the key  issues is not that people don't care or  they don't think about the issue  but they are concerned that if they give  an analgesic  it can interact with their research  protocol and confound their research  data  and since the only reason we've  justified using animals for this purpose  um is to get good data  that would indeed be a big problem  but i don't think it's an insurmountable  problem  our policy here at newcastle is always  to give at least one dose of analgesic  to give it preemptively so we have the  analgesic working before the animal  recovers it is possible  to use multimodal analgesic protocols if  we can and particularly if the surgery  allows to incorporate local anaesthetic  regimes  and then to try and use a method of pain  assessment and adjust the analgesic  and when we get to the issue of a  research worker who says i'm concerned  because i've seen that buprenorphine has  immune suppressive effects  so i don't want to use it  you take out the publication you say  yeah it does but look at the dose they  had to give to produce that effect  it's 10 or 20 fold higher than the dose  we're proposing to use for post-surgical  analgesia and if that's still a problem  well how about we use non-steroidals and  if that's still a problem what about  local anesthetics and i think there is  always a potential solution we shouldn't  have animals  not receiving anything  so i'm happy to take some questions i  want to close by thanking  the various people who funded  the individuals working at newcastle on  these  issues are part of our pain and animal  welfare science group  i also want to thank particularly  those bodies who have supported our  education activities and to highlight  the national center for the three r's  who've just funded um production of some  interactive learning resources and with  a bit of luck we'll get the url popped  up for you the first one has just been  launched which is introductory  anaesthesia it's free to use via the  nc3rs website and assist at a  second website that we run will allow  tracking of individual users and we  would love to have your feedback on it  to see how it's working and we're hard  at work on the next stage of anaesthesia  more complex anaesthetic procedures and  analgesia and pain assessment and so  forth and we hope over the next year or  so to make those available to you so  thank you very much for listening  and i'm happy to to take some questions  a second website that we will  thank you paul for that informative  presentation before we get started on  the question and answer session i would  like to remind our audience how to  submit questions you can submit  questions by typing them in the q a box  which can be found clicking on the green  q a button at the lower left of the  presentation window we will try to  answer as many questions as we can  our first question our lab conducts  mechanical ventilations on laboratory  rats to make sure they are adequately  ventilated we use a blood gas analyzer  do you believe that pulse oximeter  technology has progressed for use in the  rat to the point where it would be  almost as accurate as our current method  um  if you're ventilating your rats with um  room air  then  you will get a reasonable estimate of  how well you're ventilating  but  it won't be as accurate as blood gases  if you're ventilating with  inspired oxygens of  30 40 percent or more which is what  would be normal practice  then you won't get any indication  because the  unless you have terrible ventilation the  pulse oximeter is going to  give you high saturations  if your endotracheal tube's gone down  one bronchus and you're only ventilating  one lung then the pulse oximeter will  pick that up but what you really need is  an alternative to blood gases uh is  capnography  and  one approach that we've used  successfully in the rat  because the the difficulty with  even the newer blood gas  analyzers is the sample volume is  relatively high at least for frequent  sampling  we use um a cathode  uh we measure the entitled carbon  dioxide and we take a blood gas and we  adjust our ventilation if necessary take  another one  um and we can then follow the trend on  our catnograph and if that stays stable  that has proved to be a good reflection  of what's happening to  arterial pco2  and that avoids the need to take  multiple blood gases right the way  through and then at the very end you can  take a last one to reassure yourself  that all worked well  so i'm afraid pulse oximetry  no uh  there is a capnograph that's marketed  for rodents that will give you a really  nice accurate waveform  but it is very very temperamental in our  hands and we've tried three versions of  the of the instrument email me offline  and i'll give you more details but um i  think the approach that you've got a  blood gas analysis  maybe incorporating a capnograph would  uh  would help  now i just need to remember to pass back  to brenda  who can get the whoops who can get the  sorry brain drive i press the wrong  button  and i seem to have  really messed up here  no worries paul here i am  uh we have another question regarding  pain scoring why not evaluate muscle  contraction or body movements  so the question is to  evaluate muscle contractions and body  movements so during anaesthesia of  course we use these reflex responses um  routinely  when we're doing our clinical  observations of animals we look at body  posture and we'll look at  activity and certainly if for example  you've got a painful limb then the  animal's favoring it and it's pretty  obvious that there is an issue there  differentiate differentiating between  mechanical impairment of a limb and  pain causing reluctance to use a limb  can be difficult  and rather disappointingly  work from ourselves and others looking  at  overall activity levels  have not been particularly successful  when you do more sophisticated analyses  for example looking at circadian rhythms  that's much more helpful but i suspect  that many of those analyses are actually  looking at  more than just pain  and  i say that because often  analgesics do not normalize those  changes rapidly  and yet to all intents and purposes the  animals appear pain-free  so my hopes at the moment are  a combination of some  altered highly motivated behaviors some  abnormal behaviors and pain faces as a  way of coming up with some composite  scoring not neglecting simple things  like weighing the animal and looking at  its food and water consumption  thank you paul just as an aside so the  audience knows pdf of  dr flucknell's slides will be available  in the resource center here immediately  after the session is over you can leave  the presentation log back in and they'll  be available  our next question is  what kind of bedding material would you  recommend for housing lab animals after  surgery  so we use a product um  that was originally called vet bed um  it's also called dry bed  it's a synthetic sheepskin a synthetic  fleece i think would be my north  american translation  it's autoclavable machine washable  urine drops through it it's great for  big animals and it's great for small  animals  rats and mice will chew it but they take  a long time to destroy it and we haven't  had problems with  large numbers of fibers being ingested  and we use that a lot any of you who  have picked up on on  images that we've produced we often have  an animal sitting on on dry bed  there are alternatives but what what i  would say to avoid are sawdust and any  material that's going to stick to the  animal's  nose mouth  anus  because that's going to  make you feel uncomfortable it's going  to need to be groomed off um and of  course if it gets rubbed into its eyes  that can be a real problem so i prefer  that tissues i find pretty unsuccessful  because the animal the first thing it  seems to do is push them out of the way  i'm sure maybe someone else has got some  some other materials that they really  like but it needs to be absorbent it  needs to be non-irritant and it needs to  avoid going into the animals eyes and  mouth  thank you paul we have another question  is anybody working on biochemical  markers of pain and animals so that a  simple blood test can be developed for  pain assessment other than steroids  i'm sure there's a great deal of  activity in the pain research community  to look for biomarkers of pain and there  are  molecular markers of particular types of  pain that are being developed but what  we're interested in i think is is  something we can use  if not cage side but quickly and easily  what you might be able to to uh to do is  say okay i'm doing the same surgery the  same strain of animal for the next year  same surgeon  let's  assess the first few animals using our  hypothetical biomarker show that our  analgesic regime's working and away we  go  the problem with that is that we know  from experiencing people that the  efficacy of analgesics and the degree of  pain  varies tremendously from person to  person uh  and even if they've undergone the same  surgery with the same surgeon  and there's a concept in  analgesic clinical trials called a  number needed to treat which is the  number of patients you need to give your  drug to to get a 50 reduction in their  pain  and the number you need to give for  example oral tramadol to ranges from  four to six depending on the clinical  trial so what that means is that when we  give our standard drug  to our standard mouse after its standard  surgery  there is the likelihood that some of  those mice will need some additional  analgesics and the only way we are going  to be able to do that in a way that will  benefit those particular mice  is by assessing their pain cage side and  intervening and giving more drug so  that's why i'm keen on these these  real-time pain assessments  and i do appreciate the logistics  of that and the difficulties that can  that can produce  in a research busy research environment  thanks paul  our next question do you still encourage  oral administration with  buprophenorphene  we have a model of hemophilia in rodents  and tried morphing sr to limit  injections but the resulting hematoma  can induce more bleeding  we'd like to try one injection of that  and then p.o buprenorphine but are  getting pushed back what is your answer  what is your opinion  so the  um  giving buprenorphine orally there are  now three or four  five or six publications describing  different approaches to doing that in  drinking water in drinking water plus  supplement  in  materials like nutella and so forth  one of the problems uh is that  sometimes it's not going to work and  there are some publications from the u.s  showing that even at high doses  quite clearly in some rats you don't get  good analgesic efficacy one of my  pharmacology colleagues  suggests that this is because the  first past extraction of  rather the low bioavailability of  buprenorphine when you give it by mouth  which is  something like 90 percent to 95 percent  uh simply is is is not available um uh  to have activities and analgesic  small changes in um that fraction that's  available can have big effects on the  analgesic effect  so we've we've used it ourselves  it has attractions as with other oral  formulations of being able to give the  drug  and  then leave the animal alone uh it can it  can dose itself if you've dropped the uh  food pellet in it's habituated to that  but you need to combine that with pain  assessment and so for that reason i  preferred giving inject  thank you paul  we have another question do you have any  experience or advice using long-acting  topical fentanyl in laboratory animals  but it's worth looking at and the final  thing i'd say is assess whether you  really need um opioid level um  of pain control  after that subsequent injection uh and  is there something else you could use  although i suspect um if you're looking  at a model you're using you don't want  non-steroidals that even temporals that  even  thank you i think we're having a  technical issue  we'll have one more question  what is your thought on combining  anesthetics in rodents i.e low-dose ket  saline  subcutaneous with isoflurane  i think that's a really excellent idea  and it's a really good approach  one i'm surprised someone didn't pop up  and say we've tried ketamine metatamine  in mice it doesn't work  and there are again there are  publications out there showing that in  some strains of mice  you don't get  full planes of surgical anesthesia now  when you've given your cocktail of drugs  either intraperitoneally or  subcutaneously